{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1156340-crossover-antics","id":1156340,"title":"Crossover Antics","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-11-06T07:31:04-08:00","id":1156340,"title":"Crossover Antics","content":"Hi guys,<br /> <br /> I know the Contagion Chronicle is on the horizon now, and as excited as I am for that, I'm unable to get a regular crew around my table (hellooooooo Roll20...).<br /> <br /> So I was wondering if any of ye had had experience with running interconnected games. Not true Crossover as such; I'm thinking of a series of one shots* that cover each game line in the 2e stable. But set in the same city/area, so there's a chance for coming across characters they've seen or even played in a previous game. I guess kind of like Netflix Marvel, minus the ultimate showdown lark. <br /> <br /> Initially, I thought a nice straightforward VtR game, which would introduce them somewhat to the line. That would likely be set in The City (wherever that is). After that story had finished, run WtF set on the outskirts of the same city, or the like, with the same players now playing a pack. <br /> <br /> And so on, and so forth. <br /> <br /> Anyone tried something like this? Experiences, thoughts, advice, ominous warnings, etc?<br /> <br /> <br /> *may not actually just be one shot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3192-baileyborough","name":"baileyborough","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"72"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-06T10:46:46-08:00","id":1156398,"title":"","content":"Well, yeah. Most of my games have been in the same city, and all of them representing the same setting (so things carry over, though the characters from Chicago have never felt a need to go to Miami).<br /> <br /> I'd strongly suggest reading the core books for second edition before doing this, and really as much as you can about a splat. There is a lot of presumptions people make about certain game lines that aren't accurate about the game as represented by the books. It's fine for people who just want their own version of whatever game, but at that point you might as well just tailor the splat to your specific wants in the first place.<br /> <br /> Anyways most of the player characters end up having at least one decent connection with someone of another game line. The last vampire game I ran had a character become the domain's liaison to the werewolves, while two others were in good with hunter conspiracies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}